{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming e952d16d. I verified the defect on current main before promising rather than trusting the description: spike/compose.yaml line 10 publishes \"3300:3000\", which Docker binds to 0.0.0.0, and the environment block sets DB_TYPE, DISABLE_REGISTRATION and ROOT_URL but no install lock. So on a fresh data volume the Gitea first-run installer is reachable from the LAN, and whoever reaches it first completes setup and becomes the administrator. DISABLE_REGISTRATION does not help, because it governs self-signup after installation, not the installer itself.\n\nTwo changes, and I expect the second to be the awkward one. Binding is straightforward: publish on loopback explicitly. Locking first-run setup means the container starts with no administrator and no way to make one through the browser, so the documented workflow has to gain a concrete step for creating that account through the Gitea CLI. Hardening the port while leaving the guide's first step impossible would be a worse outcome than the defect, so the guide and the compose file move together or not at all.\n\nI will determine the correct configuration for Gitea 1.24.6 rather than assume the variable name spells what I expect, as the request asks. If I cannot establish it from the version actually pinned, I will say so instead of shipping a plausible key.\n\nThe regression will parse the effective compose configuration and fail on either defect - a published address that is not loopback, and a first run that leaves setup open - without needing a network. Then focused tests, make docs, git diff --check, exact-head artifacts for spike and any changed docs path, and independent review before any merge.\n\nNoting this request is stale, so this promise and what follows are stale too. Noting also that it refreshes my own stale self-request 2924b769, which could never have closed as filed."}